  "result": "I added `split(cents, parts)` to `shop/money.py`, and the full test suite passes: 12 tests ran, all OK. Nothing is committed.\n\n- **How it splits:** each part gets `cents // parts`, and the leftover cents go one each to the first parts. So `split(10, 3)` gives `[4, 3, 3]` and `split(11, 3)` gives `[4, 4, 3]`. If `parts` is below 1, it raises `ValueError`.\n- **Tests:** there was no test file for `money.py`, so I created `tests/test_money.py` using `unittest`, like the other test files. It has six tests:\n  - an even split\n  - the leftover cents going to the first parts\n  - the parts summing to `cents`\n  - `parts=1`\n  - `parts=0` raising an error\n  - negative `parts` raising an error\n- **Test command:** `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t .` (from `README.md`).\n\nYou didn't specify negative `cents`, and there's no test for it. Python's integer division rounds down, so `split(-10, 3)` returns `[-3, -3, -4]`. The parts still add up to the total, but the extra cent lands on the last part, not the first. Tell me if you'd prefer to reject negative amounts or handle them another way.",
